    "q-66137": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66137",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given $f(x)=2x+1$ and $g(x)=-3x+5,$ find $(f+g)(4).$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2(4)+1=9 \\\\ g(4) & =-3(4)+5=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(4)=f(4)+g(4)=9+(-7)=2\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(2x+1)+(-3x+5) \\\\  & =-x+6 \\\\  & =6-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(4) & =6-4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
